---
description: Auto-Commit Standards
globs: *.*
---
# Auto-Commit Standards

Standards for automatically committing changes of significant complexity after AI-generated code.

<rule>
name: auto_commit_significant_changes
description: Automatically commits changes of significant complexity after AI-generated code to maintain version history
filters:
  # Match code generation or file edit events
  - type: event
    pattern: "file_edit|file_create"
  # Match significant code changes (more than 20 lines modified)
  - type: metric
    pattern: "lines_changed > 20"

actions:
  - type: execute
    command: "git add {changed_files} && git commit -m 'AI-assisted: {description}'"
    conditions:
      - files_changed: true
        message: "Committing significant changes to version control"

  - type: suggest
    message: |
      Significant code changes have been detected. These changes will be automatically committed to the repository with the following guidelines:
      
      1. The commit message will include:
         - Prefix: "AI-assisted: "
         - Description of the changes made
      
      2. Auto-commits occur when:
         - More than 20 lines of code are modified
         - Multiple files are created or edited
         - Major architectural changes are implemented
      
      3. Before the auto-commit, you may:
         - Review the changes
         - Add additional details to the commit message
         - Opt-out of the auto-commit for this specific change

      4. Best practices for AI-assisted commits:
         - Keep commits focused on a single logical change
         - Include meaningful descriptions
         - Follow project-specific commit message conventions
         - Consider breaking very large changes into smaller, logical commits

examples:
  - input: |
      # Changes:
      - Created new React component (50 lines)
      - Updated API service (30 lines)
      - Modified database schema (15 lines)
    output: |
      Git commit created:
      "AI-assisted: Implement user autobiography response system with React component, API service and database schema updates"

metadata:
  priority: medium
  version: 1.0
</rule>

<rule>
name: semantic_commit_messages
description: Enforces the use of semantic commit messages for all commits to maintain clear and consistent version history
filters:
  # Match any git commit event
  - type: event
    pattern: "git_commit|commit"
  # Match commit message creation
  - type: action
    pattern: "create_commit_message|suggest_commit"

actions:
  - type: validate
    conditions:
      - pattern: "^(feat|fix|docs|style|refactor|perf|test|build|ci|chore|revert)(\\(\\w+\\))?: .+"
        message: "Commit message must follow semantic commit format"

  - type: suggest
    message: |
      All commits must follow the semantic commit message format:
      
      ```
      <type>(<scope>): <description>
      
      [optional body]
      
      [optional footer(s)]
      ```
      
      Types:
      - feat: A new feature
      - fix: A bug fix
      - docs: Documentation changes
      - style: Code style changes (formatting, missing semicolons, etc.)
      - refactor: Code changes that neither fix bugs nor add features
      - perf: Performance improvements
      - test: Adding or modifying tests
      - build: Build system or external dependency changes
      - ci: CI configuration changes
      - chore: Other changes that don't modify src or test files
      - revert: Reverting previous commits
      
      Guidelines:
      1. The first line should be 72 characters or less
      2. Second line should be blank
      3. Description should use imperative, present tense ("add" not "added")
      4. Include scope in parentheses for added clarity when appropriate
      5. Break down large changes into multiple commits
      
      Examples:
      - feat(auth): add user authentication system
      - fix(api): correct response format for error messages
      - docs(readme): update installation instructions
      - style: apply consistent indentation to all files

examples:
  - input: |
      # Non-semantic commit message
      Update user code and fix bugs
    output: |
      Invalid commit message. Please use semantic format:
      feat(user): update user profile functionality and fix validation bugs
  
  - input: |
      # Semantic commit message
      fix(auth): correct token validation for expired sessions
    output: |
      Valid semantic commit message

metadata:
  priority: high
  version: 1.0
</rule>